WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.560 --> 00:00:04.850
Gather 'round the maypole, friends Ari Aster's
Midsommar is nearly upon us!

00:00:04.850 --> 00:00:08.610
But before you head out to the festivities,
you may want to know a bit about what you're

00:00:08.610 --> 00:00:10.120
getting yourself into.

00:00:10.360 --> 00:00:13.480
The name Ari Aster may not ring many bells
for you.

00:00:13.480 --> 00:00:16.840
After all, Midsommar is only the second movie
he's directed.

00:00:16.840 --> 00:00:21.660
But his debut was one of 2018's best horror
films: Hereditary.

00:00:21.660 --> 00:00:25.850
Starring Toni Collette, Gabriel Byrne, and
Alex Wolff as a family faced with a sudden,

00:00:25.850 --> 00:00:30.890
horrible tragedy, it's a slow burn of a film
that ratchets up the tension all the way through

00:00:30.890 --> 00:00:33.540
before unleashing hell in its last act.

00:00:33.540 --> 00:00:38.230
Hereditary was such a total package that there
was even some "Best Actress" Oscar buzz for

00:00:38.230 --> 00:00:39.230
Collette.

00:00:39.230 --> 00:00:42.540
She didn't receive a nomination, but the fact
that she was even in consideration should

00:00:42.540 --> 00:00:46.040
tell you something about the kind of "oomph"
that the movie is packing.

00:00:46.040 --> 00:00:50.870
Don't let Aster's small filmography fool you:
if Midsommar can shake audiences like Hereditary

00:00:50.880 --> 00:00:55.560
did, he'll seal his reputation as a filmmaker
who's impossible to ignore.

00:00:55.560 --> 00:00:59.750
Midsommar's trailer makes it clear that this
is not a movie for the faint of heart.

00:00:59.750 --> 00:01:02.540
It looks intense, frightening, and unsettling.

00:01:02.540 --> 00:01:06.190
But the director has a few other ways to describe
it that may surprise you.

00:01:06.190 --> 00:01:11.100
In a discussion with Vulture, Aster described
Midsommar as, quote, "a breakup movie, in

00:01:11.100 --> 00:01:13.640
the same way that Hereditary was a family
tragedy."

00:01:13.640 --> 00:01:22.000
"It's an operatic breakup movie and a dark
contemporary fairy tale."

00:01:22.000 --> 00:01:26.290
He also expressed his desire for the movie
to be viewed on its own merits those who go

00:01:26.290 --> 00:01:29.930
in expecting the same thing as Hereditary
are going to be disappointed.

00:01:29.930 --> 00:01:34.109
As if to make that distinction clearer, he
went on to describe the movie as, quote, "The

00:01:34.109 --> 00:01:36.039
Wizard of Oz for perverts."

00:01:36.039 --> 00:01:39.750
That description alone should be a pretty
good indicator of whether or not Midsommar

00:01:39.750 --> 00:01:41.700
is the kind of movie you should see.

00:01:41.700 --> 00:01:44.399
"Did I scare you?"

00:01:44.399 --> 00:01:47.399
“No, no, I just thought you hurt yourself."

00:01:47.399 --> 00:01:49.210
"But I didn't scare you?"

00:01:49.210 --> 00:01:51.360
"No, of course not."

00:01:52.040 --> 00:01:56.520
Most of Midsommar's cast is made up of talented
young actors who you may not yet recognize

00:01:56.540 --> 00:01:57.639
by name.

00:01:57.639 --> 00:02:01.520
Florence Pugh and Jack Reynor take the lead
roles as Dani and Christian.

00:02:01.520 --> 00:02:07.259
Pugh is probably most recognizable from her
role as Katherine in the 2016 film Lady Macbeth,

00:02:07.259 --> 00:02:11.180
but she also appears in films like Outlaw
King, Fighting With My Family, and Marvel's

00:02:11.180 --> 00:02:13.250
upcoming Black Widow film.

00:02:13.250 --> 00:02:17.560
You might recognize Reynor's voice from the
Andy Serkis-directed Mowgli: Legend of the

00:02:17.560 --> 00:02:19.830
Jungle, where he played Brother Wolf.

00:02:19.830 --> 00:02:24.390
He also appeared in the film Grassland alongside
Toni Collette and another one of Midsommar's

00:02:24.390 --> 00:02:25.670
stars, Will Poulter.

00:02:25.670 --> 00:02:30.129
Poulter plays the character Mark, and you
probably recognize him as Colin from the interactive

00:02:30.129 --> 00:02:34.579
Netflix feature Black Mirror: Bandersnatch
though he also played a prominent role in

00:02:34.579 --> 00:02:35.959
the Maze Runner series.

00:02:35.959 --> 00:02:39.079
Finally, William Jackson Harper plays Josh.

00:02:39.079 --> 00:02:43.209
He's best known as conflicted ethics professor
Chidi Anagonye on The Good Place.

00:02:43.209 --> 00:02:47.530
We'll have to see if his inability to solve
the Trolley Problem comes up in Midsommar.

00:02:47.530 --> 00:02:49.709
"Why don't you just tell me the right answer?"

00:02:49.709 --> 00:02:54.879
"Well that's what's so great about the trolley
problem is there is no right answer."

00:02:54.879 --> 00:02:56.049
Broad daylight

00:02:56.049 --> 00:02:59.910
Audiences are used to scary films being set
in the dark, tricking them with shadows and

00:02:59.910 --> 00:03:03.200
obscuring the horrors lurking just outside
of their field of vision.

00:03:03.200 --> 00:03:07.700
It takes just a quick glance at the marketing
for Midsommar to realize that this film operates

00:03:07.700 --> 00:03:09.189
a little bit differently.

00:03:09.189 --> 00:03:12.080
Everything is very bright too bright, almost.

00:03:12.080 --> 00:03:15.819
The mood seems scary but the look is all sunlight
and pastels.

00:03:15.819 --> 00:03:18.819
It makes for an eerie sense of dissonance.

00:03:18.819 --> 00:03:22.870
Even a minor detail, like the letterbox of
the trailer being white instead of the usual

00:03:22.870 --> 00:03:25.629
black, is off-putting and unnerving.

00:03:25.629 --> 00:03:29.489
That lighting scheme plays into the themes
and plot of the actual movie, which takes

00:03:29.489 --> 00:03:31.950
place in an environment of eternal sunlight.

00:03:31.950 --> 00:03:33.310
"What time is it?"

00:03:33.310 --> 00:03:34.540
"9 pm."

00:03:34.540 --> 00:03:38.810
The bright imagery seems like it will lend
itself well to an otherworldly feel, especially

00:03:38.810 --> 00:03:40.900
as the plot turns increasingly sinister.

00:03:40.900 --> 00:03:45.600
It will also make it increasingly difficult
for the protagonists to find a place to hide.

00:03:46.120 --> 00:03:49.069
So, what's really going on in Midsommar?

00:03:49.069 --> 00:03:53.410
According to the official synopsis, it follows
Dani and Christian, a young American couple

00:03:53.410 --> 00:03:55.170
on the verge of breaking up.

00:03:55.170 --> 00:03:59.269
Following a tragedy in Dani's family, she
ends up on a trip with Christian and his friends

00:03:59.269 --> 00:04:03.459
to a momentous midsummer festival in an isolated
village in Sweden.

00:04:03.459 --> 00:04:07.860
Going on a vacation to fix a broken relationship
is, in real life, a terrible idea.

00:04:07.860 --> 00:04:12.319
But in the context of the movie, it adds a
great deal to the sense of conflict and emotional

00:04:12.319 --> 00:04:14.230
tension between the characters.

00:04:14.230 --> 00:04:18.320
This is probably why Ari Aster describes Midsommar
as a "breakup movie."

00:04:18.320 --> 00:04:22.960
It's just going to be an even more unpleasant
breakup than we may have ever seen before.

00:04:23.280 --> 00:04:28.080
Much of Midsommar's aesthetic comes from traditional
Scandanavian holiday rituals.

00:04:28.080 --> 00:04:32.240
These midsummer celebrations take place on
a Friday in June, in commemoration of the

00:04:32.259 --> 00:04:33.850
longest day of the year.

00:04:33.850 --> 00:04:38.110
The traditions date back to pagan rituals
designed to win the favor of the sun god.

00:04:38.110 --> 00:04:40.900
The Midsommar festival is a big deal in Sweden.

00:04:40.900 --> 00:04:45.240
Typical decorations involve plenty of flower
garlands and wreaths, and businesses shut

00:04:45.240 --> 00:04:47.790
down so that people can have the entire day
to celebrate.

00:04:47.790 --> 00:04:49.100
"Right now it's summer in Sweden.

00:04:49.100 --> 00:04:51.600
Are you gonna go back and celebrate and hang
out?"

00:04:51.600 --> 00:04:53.600
"Probably our biggest holiday of the year.

00:04:53.610 --> 00:04:56.060
It's bigger than New Year's, called Midsommar."

00:04:56.060 --> 00:05:01.280
Maypoles for dancing and gigantic bonfires
are common, and as the festivities wind down,

00:05:01.280 --> 00:05:05.699
there is often a reading of fortunes so people
can determine who they will marry in the future.

00:05:05.699 --> 00:05:10.389
Obviously, the celebration in the film isn't
exactly like these traditional rites.

00:05:10.389 --> 00:05:14.669
But those traditions do serve as a clear inspiration
for the more horrifying rituals the movie

00:05:14.680 --> 00:05:15.680
has in store.

00:05:16.040 --> 00:05:20.320
If Midsommar proves successful, you might
start to see Ari Aster's name thrown around

00:05:20.330 --> 00:05:22.259
as a new "master of horror."

00:05:22.259 --> 00:05:26.000
But if you're expecting him to follow this
film up with another horror feature, you probably

00:05:26.000 --> 00:05:27.650
shouldn't hold your breath.

00:05:27.650 --> 00:05:31.210
When IndieWire spoke with Aster about the
film, the director made his intentions for

00:05:31.210 --> 00:05:33.280
future projects clear, saying,

00:05:33.280 --> 00:05:35.949
"[Midsommar] is the only other horror movie
I have.

00:05:35.949 --> 00:05:38.340
And I'm pretty sure that's going to be it
for a long time.

00:05:38.340 --> 00:05:43.030
I love the genre, I consider myself a genre
filmmaker in that I want to play in every

00:05:43.030 --> 00:05:44.030
genre.

00:05:44.030 --> 00:05:45.180
I would love to make a musical."

00:05:45.180 --> 00:05:49.310
He also shared that he's in the process of
writing a sci-fi film, and has four or five

00:05:49.310 --> 00:05:52.639
movies from his backlog of scripts, quote,
"ready to go."

00:05:52.639 --> 00:05:56.620
He isn't saying that he'll never return to
horror, but it does seem like, if he has any

00:05:56.620 --> 00:05:59.280
say in the matter, it won't happen again for
a while.

00:05:59.680 --> 00:06:02.880
Horror movies usually have fairly brief runtimes.

00:06:02.900 --> 00:06:06.840
After all, it can be a challenge to keep upping
the stakes of a story without exhausting the

00:06:06.840 --> 00:06:09.539
audience to the point of checking out.

00:06:09.539 --> 00:06:10.620
Midsommar is an exception.

00:06:10.620 --> 00:06:14.900
You'll probably want to hit the bathroom before
settling in to watch it, since the movie sports

00:06:14.900 --> 00:06:17.340
a runtime of two hours and 20 minutes.

00:06:17.340 --> 00:06:18.990
"That sounds fun."

00:06:18.990 --> 00:06:23.530
Even Hereditary couldn't quite slow burn its
way to that length that movie is 13 minutes

00:06:23.530 --> 00:06:25.680
shorter than Midsommar's final cut.

00:06:25.680 --> 00:06:30.000
So don't go into this movie expecting something
simple or easy to digest.

00:06:30.000 --> 00:06:33.990
Like many of the great horror movies, it's
not something to just sit back and watch it's

00:06:34.000 --> 00:06:35.720
an experience to endure.

00:06:36.240 --> 00:06:40.470
If you like your horror movies with loud noises
and obvious jump scares, you may want to steer

00:06:40.470 --> 00:06:45.520
clear from Midsommar, a film with a director
who hesitates to even refer to it as a horror

00:06:45.520 --> 00:06:46.520
movie.

00:06:46.520 --> 00:06:50.880
In a conversation with Fandango, Aster danced
around questions about just how scary Midsommar

00:06:50.880 --> 00:06:52.060
is, saying,

00:06:52.060 --> 00:06:55.610
"It's really leaning more on suspense than
it is scares.

00:06:55.610 --> 00:06:57.510
In some ways, it's more surreal.

00:06:57.510 --> 00:07:00.050
[...] This is an adult contemporary fairy
tale."

00:07:00.050 --> 00:07:04.129
It's worth keeping in mind that fairy tales
can be pretty horrifying in their own right.

00:07:04.129 --> 00:07:08.789
If you don't believe us, go check out Grimm's
fairy tales or, for a more modern take, many

00:07:08.789 --> 00:07:10.810
of the films of Guillermo del Toro.

00:07:10.810 --> 00:07:15.170
And remember, just because Midsommar doesn't
look like a horror film doesn't mean it won't

00:07:15.170 --> 00:07:17.340
still scare the living daylights out of you.

